  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 AM MDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nIt has been an active few months, with everything from all-time\nrecord heat to a monsoon that seemed like it never wanted to \nend (it looks like it has finally ended, thankfully), with \nseemingly something happening each day. You know it wouldn't be \none of my discussions without an old movie reference referring \nto the weather pattern. And today, we turn to an 80s movie \nstarring the late, great Rodney Dangerfield with an assist from \nJoe Pesci, the movie is Easy Money. While the weather will \nlikely not be completely quiet, the general pattern will be, at\nleast compared to many of the previous weeks, Easy Money for \nmeteorologists.\n\nDrier air is currently pressing southward into the area from the \nnorthwest. This should bring a pleasant, early autumn day to most of \nthe area. The exception may be Sweetwater County, especially south \nof Interstate 80. Just enough moisture may remain there and they may \nbe on the edge of the moisture from the remnants of Hurricane Polo \nfor there to be a few showers, especially in the morning. I also \ncan't rule out an isolated shower or thunderstorm here in the \nafternoon, but with the chance less than 1 out of 10, we left the \nforecast dry for now. Most locations should be comfortably warm, \nwith highs generally 5 to 10 degrees above normal. Wednesday also \nlooks like a nice autumn day as just about all guidance keeps the \nmoisture from Polo to the south across Colorado.\n\nThings could get a little more active on Thursday as a cold front \ndrops in from the north. The front is of continental origin and \ndoesn't have a ton of moisture to work with though. One thing it \nwill have is a jet streak moving along it as it drops \nsouthward. This may bring enough lift to produce some showers \nThursday or Thursday night. Most guidance shows this largely \nnorth of Wyoming, but if the front can drop a bit further south,\nit could impact portions of Big Horn and Johnson Counties with \nsome showers. PoPs are low for now though, generally less than \n1 out of 3. Wind may also need to be increased a bit with the \ncold advection pattern, but high wind is not expected. The main \nimpact will be to knock temperatures down somewhat, especially \nin a place like Buffalo which could be 10 to 15 degrees cooler \nthan on Wednesday. Most locations east of the Divide will \ngenerally be 5 to 10 degrees cooler on Thursday and possibly \nFriday as well. \n\nAnother front may get close to Wyoming Friday night or \nSaturday, but this one is even drier and would only produce very\nisolated showers, again mainly across northern Wyoming. \nFollowing that, a vast majority of guidance then rebuilds a \nstrong ridge across the western Unites States, bringing a \nprolonged period of above normal temperatures with mainly dry \nconditions. Some guidance is showing a shortwave possibly \nproducing a few showers on Monday, but this is highly uncertain \nthis far out. So, other than a couple of weak systems, the \nweather looks relatively uneventful across the area. And, there \nare no signs of a big cooldown with temperatures generally \nremaining near to above normal over the next several days.",
